#!/bin/sh
# The cheap checks that have each caught a shipped-would-have-been bug:
#   trace_load     a top-level throw silently kills ALL of app.js (2 catches)
#   test_timeline  the maths that places every frame
#   test_watchdog  the /queue parse that decides whether a stalled job is killed
#   test_workflow  the graphs ComfyUI is actually asked to render
# smoke_api and test_apimode need the server running, so they stay manual —
# a hook that fails whenever the app is closed is a hook people delete.
# The server's own entry parses. trace_load evaluates the CLIENT's app.js and
# says nothing about server/index.js, so a merge that duplicated a top-level
# declaration there passed every check and was only caught by trying to boot
# the app by hand. One syntax check closes that hole for the price of nothing.
node --check server/index.js || exit 1
node scripts/trace_load.mjs || exit 1
node scripts/test_timeline.mjs || exit 1
node scripts/test_watchdog.mjs || exit 1
node scripts/test_workflow.mjs || exit 1
# The 2026-08-26 matrix findings that live in plain node: the engine-adoption
# guard (a neighbour ComfyUI on our port must be a startup ERROR, never
# adopted) and wait_for_song's unknown-id fast fail.
node scripts/test_matrixfix.mjs || exit 1
node server/vfx/templates_test.js || exit 1
node server/vfx/store_test.js || exit 1
node server/vfx/routes_test.js || exit 1
node server/mcp-vfx_test.js || exit 1
# The DAW's store maths (meter maps, bar-derived timeline, the dirty-region
# hashes), its route surface, and the daw_* declared-and-dropped guard.
node server/daw/store_test.js || exit 1
node server/daw/routes_test.js || exit 1
node server/mcp-daw_test.js || exit 1
# [DAWREC] The capture path's maths: chunk assembly (sample-exact across
# boundaries), latency-offset placement, punch trim, comp flattening, the
# meter-aware count-in (7/8 counts in 7), audio clips in the region hashes,
# and ACTOR HONESTY at the capture seam (an agent-driven record can never
# log as a human performance).
node server/daw/capture_test.js || exit 1
# The mixer/rack document model and THE DEPENDENCY-GRAPH RULE: a default
# mixer leaves every hash byte-identical to P0's; stateful chains reach
# forward; returns dirty their consumers; sidechain drags its source along.
node server/daw/mixer_test.js || exit 1
# [DAWUI] The arrangement window's static gates: THE PARITY GATE (every
# action the page posts is one routes.js/mixer.js really dispatches - the
# "one document, two hands" rule in executable form), the top-level-binding
# id check for the new zones, the dB fader law, the three keymap profiles,
# the catalog-driven device panel (no device parameter is hard-coded), the
# live-sync frame, and the one-palette rule for web/daw.css.
node server/daw/ui_test.js || exit 1
# THE EAR's loop machinery: the critique->edit mapping validated against the
# REAL MCP tool schemas and the rack catalog (a card that promises an edit the
# tool would refuse is worse than no card), routes that are distinct OUTCOMES
# rather than intensity variants, the A/B guard actually calling its undo, and
# ACTOR HONESTY at the decision seam - `choice` refuses an agent actor and
# `judge` refuses a user one, so no argument anyone can pass records an AI
# decision as a human one. Do not thin: this suite is the invariant.
node server/daw/ear_test.js || exit 1
# The same declared-and-dropped guard, over the main tool list — plus the
# structural checks for the routes the image panels lean on.
node server/mcp-image_test.js || exit 1
# The provenance ledger: chain integrity, ACTOR HONESTY (an MCP-driven action
# can never record as a human one — the invariant every dossier stands on),
# the origin fold, and the source pins that keep the Tier-1 AI marker
# unstrippable via any exposed option. Art-50 compliance layer; do not thin.
node server/provenance_test.js || exit 1
# The NOTICE file's model list is GENERATED from server/models.js. That section
# is how a licence travels with a fork, and the hand-kept version had already
# gone stale in the worst direction — it omitted Ideogram 4, the one entry whose
# output terms nobody has read. This fails the commit if the catalogue moved and
# the file did not; the fix is `node scripts/gen_notice.mjs`.
node scripts/gen_notice.mjs --check || exit 1
# The imagetools tests need a python with PIL/numpy/scipy — the ComfyUI venv
# has all three. Guarded on its presence for the same reason the server tests
# stay manual: a hook that fails on a machine without the rig gets deleted.
PY="${AIPLAY_RIG:-D:/AI/aiplay-studio-bench}/venv/Scripts/python.exe"
if [ -x "$PY" ]; then "$PY" server/imagetools_test.py || exit 1; fi
# imgselect decides where every other op lands, so a regression here is a
# regression in all 25 adjustments and all 75 effects at once.
if [ -x "$PY" ]; then "$PY" server/imgselect_test.py || exit 1; fi
if [ -x "$PY" ]; then "$PY" server/imgstroke_test.py || exit 1; fi
if [ -x "$PY" ]; then "$PY" server/imgtext_test.py || exit 1; fi
# imgdoc composites THROUGH engine.py rather than owning a copy of the blend
# maths, and its blend list is parsed straight out of store.js — so this suite
# is also the alarm for a compositor rename or a blend-mode added on one side.
if [ -x "$PY" ]; then "$PY" server/imgdoc_test.py || exit 1; fi
# Shapes, canvas and geometry. Almost every case is arithmetic - 800 pixels,
# pi*a*b, a stroke that measures its own width - so a rasteriser that drifts by
# a fraction of a pixel fails here rather than in someone's eye six weeks later.
if [ -x "$PY" ]; then "$PY" server/imgshape_test.py || exit 1; fi
# Paths and liquify. Same argument as imgshape: a pen tool has a closed-form
# answer for everything - w*L of ink, pi*r^2 of area, a 20px push that moves a
# dot 20px - so a rasteriser or a displacement field that drifts fails here
# rather than in someone's eye. It also pins the two invariants that hide: a
# frozen pixel is BIT-identical, and straight alpha survives the warp (it
# checks the wrong way round too, so the guard cannot quietly stop working).
if [ -x "$PY" ]; then "$PY" server/imgpath_test.py || exit 1; fi
# imgexport writes real files in seven formats and reads every one of them back,
# so it is the slowest suite here — around 17s, and it prints where that went
# per section. It earns it: the encoder bugs it covers (a silently 3-of-4-entry
# ICO, a "lossless" WebP that rewrites transparent pixels, EXIF surviving a
# strip) are all invisible until somebody opens the file somewhere else.
if [ -x "$PY" ]; then "$PY" server/imgexport_test.py || exit 1; fi
# imgphoto_test ends with a 4096x4096 timing pass over all eight photo tools —
# about ten seconds, and it is the only thing that would notice one of them
# turning into a minute.
if [ -x "$PY" ]; then "$PY" server/imgphoto_test.py || exit 1; fi
if [ -x "$PY" ]; then "$PY" server/timelinemix_test.py || exit 1; fi
if [ -x "$PY" ]; then "$PY" server/vfx/engine_test.py || exit 1; fi
if [ -x "$PY" ]; then "$PY" server/vfx/effects_test.py || exit 1; fi
# Particles: the closed form against RK4, cross-process sha1 determinism,
# scrub independence, the birth-integral, the caps. The sweeps above prove the
# pixel contract; this suite proves the particle system is RIGHT.
if [ -x "$PY" ]; then "$PY" server/vfx/particles_test.py || exit 1; fi
if [ -x "$PY" ]; then "$PY" server/vfx/expressions_test.py || exit 1; fi
# expressions_test proves the sandbox; this one proves it reaches the pixels.
# Both, because the sandbox passed 100% for as long as the engine ignored it.
if [ -x "$PY" ]; then "$PY" server/vfx/expressions_engine_test.py || exit 1; fi
if [ -x "$PY" ]; then "$PY" server/vfx/shapes_test.py || exit 1; fi
# Lights. Same bargain as shapes: everything a light does has a closed form -
# Lambert, inverse square, h*tan(cone/2), and a shadow whose umbra is similar
# triangles - so the suite predicts whole fields and compares arrays. It also
# prints the 1080p cost of every light type on every run, which is the only
# thing that would notice shading a layer turning into a second.
if [ -x "$PY" ]; then "$PY" server/vfx/lights_test.py || exit 1; fi
if [ -x "$PY" ]; then "$PY" server/vfx/integration_test.py || exit 1; fi
# Audio → notes. The floors are the AMT eval's measured results (F1 0.93/0.914/
# 0.80/0.88, 12/12 chords, 3/3 bends, 100% playable fingering) re-proven per
# commit against synthesised ground truth; the transcription sections skip by
# themselves when basic-pitch is not in the venv, the refusal path always runs.
if [ -x "$PY" ]; then "$PY" server/vfx/notes_test.py || exit 1; fi
# The DAW engine: synth determinism, the bit-exact region-seam proof, and the
# P0-4 loopback estimator (+-1 ms on synthetic offsets 3-250 ms).
if [ -x "$PY" ]; then "$PY" server/daw/engine_test.py || exit 1; fi
# [DAWREC] The capture codecs and the engine's audio mixing: the FLAC round
# trip at the 24-bit floor, encode/decode scale inverses ACROSS files, the
# region-seam proof extended to file-backed clips, sample-exact clip
# placement and trim, and the click landing blips exactly where told.
if [ -x "$PY" ]; then "$PY" server/daw/capture_test.py || exit 1; fi
# The DAW rack: per-device DSP proofs (bypass bit-exact, EQ FFT, crest,
# limiter true-peak ceiling, tempo-grid delay, sidechain duck, vfx-keyframe
# automation), the full-graph seam/determinism pin, BS.1770 metering against
# its own calibration constant, and the per-device CPU budget.
if [ -x "$PY" ]; then "$PY" server/daw/rack_test.py || exit 1; fi
# The MASTERING SUITE. Everything here is a claim a wrong device would still
# make: LP4+HP4 measured against the closed-form allpass, the band tree
# summing back, the M/S butterfly bit-exact on float32-sourced audio, a
# multiband band ducking ITS band and nothing else, the maximizer holding a
# true-peak ceiling under a slammed input at every character, a -100 dBFS
# tone surviving a dithered 16-bit reduction and dying without it, and
# device_response matched against the device's own MEASURED impulse response.
# It also pins the master-bus finding (rack.py's tanh sits downstream of every
# insert, so a ceiling knob is not a file's ceiling) and prints the CPU table.
if [ -x "$PY" ]; then "$PY" server/daw/master_test.py || exit 1; fi
# THE EAR's objective critic, against SYNTHESISED GROUND TRUTH: a mix built so
# the pad masks the vocal in a known band over known bars, a track rendered
# exactly 6 dB down, a bounce with a known number of pinned samples, a
# mono-summed file whose side energy is exactly zero, pink noise on the pink
# reference. A critic that drifts fails here rather than in someone's ears.
# It also pins the honest absence of the aesthetic judge - no fabricated score.
if [ -x "$PY" ]; then "$PY" server/daw/ear_test.py || exit 1; fi
# The instrument stage: the stereo seam contract on every patch, the four
# generate-this-part refusals, the SFZ subset against the packs we ship, and
# DETERMINISM across cache, note order AND processes (FluidSynth is history-
# dependent unless each note gets a fresh synth - this suite is what proves
# the fix still holds). Pack-dependent sections skip themselves when the
# samples are not installed, so it runs on a machine that never downloaded.
if [ -x "$PY" ]; then "$PY" server/daw/instruments_test.py || exit 1; fi
# The embed round trip, with INDEPENDENT readers: PNG chunks and JPEG segments
# walked by hand, audio read back through ffmpeg's demuxer. Proves the marker
# survives every metadata mode ("none" included) and the Tier-2 toggle strips
# only the record — the behavioural half of the Tier-1 pin above.
if [ -x "$PY" ]; then "$PY" server/provenance_embed_test.py || exit 1; fi
